      "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7652926/2026/10/02/tony-romo-cbs-sports-part-ways-nfl-coverage-dui",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-16T19:27:34.535Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7652926/2026/10/02/tony-romo-cbs-sports-part-ways-nfl-coverage-dui/",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=utf-8",
      "pageTitle": "Tony Romo, CBS Sports part ways after July OWI arrest",
      "pageDescription": "Romo was placed on indefinite leave shortly after the arrest, and his place on CBS&#x27; No. 1 NFL broadcast team was taken by J.J. Watt.",
      "evidenceText": "Tony Romo, CBS Sports part ways after July OWI arrest - The Athletic NFL Latest LIVE Reaction & Analysis 7m ago Wake-Up Call Sando's Pick Six Maye's Significant Injury Burrow's MVP Statement Vikings Are Contenders Again Tony Romo, CBS Sports part ways after July OWI arrest Tony Romo said in a statement Friday that he is “proud” of what he accomplished at CBS Sports over the past nine years. Noam Galai / Getty Images By Andrew Marchand and Mark Puleo Oct. 2, 2026 Updated Share article 830 Tony Romo and CBS Sports have “mutually agreed to part ways,” the network said in a statement Friday. The announcement comes just over two months after Romo was arrested on suspicion of operating a vehicle while intoxicated (OWI) in Milwaukee. Romo was placed on indefinite leave shortly after the arrest, and his place on CBS’ No. 1 NFL broadcast team was taken by J.J. Watt. That arrangement will remain in place, CBS Sports said in Friday’s announcement. Advertisement “We thank Tony for his contributions over the past nine years and wish him the best moving forward,” the network said in its statement. The 46-year-old Romo had four years and $72 million remaining on the 10-year, $180 million contract he signed with CBS in 2020. CBS Sports and Romo reached a financial settlement before Friday’s announcement, according to industry sources who spoke on condition of anonymity because the negotiations were private. The two sides avoided going to court, indicating that Romo will likely be paid a good portion of the remaining money he was owed. Romo, who said in a statement Friday that he is “proud” of what he accomplished at CBS Sports, worked alongside Jim Nantz since 2017. The pair called three Super Bowls together, most recently in 2024, and Nantz had said he hoped the two would become the “modern-day version” of Pat Summerall and John Madden. “I’m going to use this time to take a break, something I’ve never done before, and focus on my health and my family before moving on to my next opportunity,” Romo, the former Dallas Cowboys quarterback, said in a statement released shortly after CBS’ announcement Friday. “I am grateful for my time with CBS Sports and for the continued support of my family and friends, as well as the NFL community and fans.” Advertisement CBS Sports president David Berson said in mid-August at the annual NFL Media Day that “no timetable or final decision” had been made on Romo’s future with the company. In early September, Romo shared on Instagram that he was pleading no contest to a civil offense of OWI, adding: “I own this completely.” He mentioned later in that statement that he was turning his attention to “my health, my family, and in time, my return to CBS.” An arrest report obtained by The Athletic stated that Romo performed poorly on standardized field sobriety tests after he was pulled over for unsafe passing and was taken into custody on July 23. Romo, whose blood alcohol level of 0.15 was nearly twice the legal limit in Wisconsin, was also found with an “open bottle of an alcoholic beverage” in his car, according to documents obtained from the Milwaukee County Sheriff’s Office. Nov 16, 2026 Connections: Sports Edition Spot the pattern. Connect the terms Find the hidden link between sports terms Play today's puzzle Tagged To: NFL Sports Business",
