      "id": "6748ad30601c79a87fb661d42d1f3428098da62224371f1e7416c96a500de4ff",
      "title": "What happened at Pete Hegseth’s first press conference in months",
      "url": "https://www.poynter.org/commentary/2026/how-to-pitch-create-new-journalism-job",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-16T19:27:34.535Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://www.poynter.org/commentary/2026/how-to-pitch-create-new-journalism-job/",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "Want a newsroom job that doesn&#039;t exist? Here&#039;s how to pitch it. - Poynter",
      "pageDescription": "You don’t have to wait for the perfect opening. Here’s how to identify a need, propose a new role and convince newsroom leaders to take a chance.",
      "evidenceText": "Want a newsroom job that doesn't exist? Here's how to pitch it. - Poynter Want a newsroom job that doesn't exist? Here's how to pitch it. - Poynter DONATE What do you want to search? Submit Sign In | Membership News Training Ethics Fact-Checking Media Literacy AI Events Future of Public Media Newmark Ethics Center IFCN MediaWise PolitiFact Donate About Us Press Freedom Watch The Poynter 50 Podcasts Artificial Intelligence Business & Work Reporting & Editing Fact-Checking Ethics & Trust Local News Journalism, Media & Communication Training Poynter Membership Categories Consulting & Coaching For ACES Members For Local News Funders All Categories Broadcast & Visual Journalism Business & Work Ethics Fact-Checking & Media Literacy Leadership Reporting & Editing Writing Poynter ACES Introductory Certificate in Editing Poynter ACES Intermediate Certificate in Editing Poynter ACES Advanced Certificate in Editing Workshops and Coaching Tools for Funders Ethics Ethics Training Ethics Articles AI Ethics Public Editor Project Get Ethics Advice Fact-Checking Articles IFCN IFCN Grants International Fact-Checking Day Teen Creator Network PolitiFact MediaWise Media Literacy Resources AI Literacy Ambassadors Teen Creator Network Research & News International en Español MediaWise Training All Events The 2026 Poynter Journalism Prizes Business & Work Commentary (Shutterstock/Olga_TG) By: Cristina Silva November 10, 2026 A mid-career reporter came to me recently with a career dilemma: She knew exactly what she wanted to do next, but that job didn’t exist at her newsroom. After nearly a decade on the education beat, the reporter wanted to move into a role that let her use her burgeoning AI skills to help other education reporters tackle complex stories. Do you know any newsrooms looking for someone to do this kind of work? she asked. No, I told her, but why not start meeting with editors to explain why they should create this role for you? For journalists who know what they want but not exactly how to do it within the limits of today’s newsrooms, drafting a new job description and selling it to the bosses is a bold move. But it can be the most direct path to getting what you want — if you do it successfully. Many journalists are passive about their career moves, waiting for a job opening or a manager to suggest their next role. Many newsroom leaders, meanwhile, are trying to ensure they have the right people in the most strategic roles while finding room in already tight budgets for experimentation, innovation and audience growth. If you believe you could fill a critical unmet need, stepping up with a solution and a detailed plan could be just what your organization needs. To make that pitch, first think critically about what needs your company has that aren’t being served. Consider your skills, how you’d like to spend your time and what you can do to address the gap. That could mean bringing back the immigration beat or narrative editor job that has been vacant for eight years; suggesting a position your newsroom has never had before, such as TikTok video host or new audiences editor; or dreaming up an entirely new job based on an emerging industry, trend or technology, such as AI product manager or a beat covering smart manufacturing and physical AI. Next, write out your pitch to refine your thinking and give the bosses a clear roadmap for how the job would function. Propose a job title and write up a job description that explains what daily, weekly and long-term success looks like. As with any position you’re pursuing, explain why you are the best person to tackle this work. Set up meetings with the right people to discuss your idea. Prepare your strongest arguments and anticipate the questions or concerns your bosses might raise. Newsroom leaders are likely to consider a new role only if it makes the newsroom stronger. So frame your pitch through that critical lens. Explain how your role will grow audience, improve newsroom culture or efficiency, provide stronger coverage of a competitive topic, or accomplish some other major need. You don’t have to immediately go all in. You can ask your boss for a three-month trial, or to spend two days a week on the proposed work while continuing your primary responsibilities. Think about whether someone can cover for you and what it would mean for your organization if you spent less time on your regular duties. Let’s say you’re the politics reporter. Proposing a trial period in the weeks before a big election is probably not the best move. Suggesting you start at the beginning of summer, when perhaps an intern could fill in for you, could make more sense. There is another approach to getting the job you want when it doesn’t exist: Just start doing some of the work you want to do when you have time after doing your actual job. After you’ve racked up some wins, you can go to the bosses and say, I am already doing this job. How about we make this my full-time gig? There are limits to this: Don’t get stuck in a situation where you end up having to do the work of two people for one salary. And if you go down this road, remember that you cannot slack off in the job you’ve been assigned while you tackle your new interests. Doing so could raise questions about your performance and hurt your relationships in the newsroom. Whatever approach you take, a yes depends on a number of factors, including your reputation for delivering. The bosses will likely consider things beyond your control, such as the company’s size, immediate goals and overall mission statement, as well as industry trends. There is recent precedent for this kind of career strategy: Look at all the journalists working in digital roles, from bloggers to newsletter editors to SEO editors to homepage editors and beyond. Many of these jobs did not exist 30 years ago. As digital journalism evolved, journalists helped create and define these roles, sometimes advancing quickly as they mastered new skills and responsibilities. Of course, one thing any digital journalist will tell you is that spending your time on work that perhaps your bosses don’t easily understand does not necessarily mean you are going to make more money. As you advance in your career, you should always ask for a pay increase to tackle more senior work. But if this new role is important to you and money might be a reason for your boss to say no, you might have to weigh whether you can move forward without a pay increase. Is getting new skills and experiences worth it for you? At times, I’ve taken pay cuts or stayed at the same salary to gain responsibilities and skills that eventually led to better opportunities and higher earnings. If your proposal is rejected by your immediate supervisor, that doesn’t mean you’ve hit a dead end. Is there someone else in the organization with a different vision, more authority or broader strategic responsibilities who you can ask for feedback? They may offer a different perspective on whether the role has potential or how you could refine your pitch. You don’t have to pitch these kinds of jobs as an internal candidate. Some editor you’ve never met before might be thrilled to hear about your brilliant idea to make their newsroom more competitive. While networking in person, over email, on Zoom or by phone, outline your vision and feel out their reaction. To be sure, this kind of career move is rarely a quick fix. It could take months or years to champion your vision and persuade an organization to make a new investment. But true innovators know to keep going, pushing open new doors and refining their ask until they get what they want. They know the org chart that exists today doesn’t have to limit their ambitions. And they know that their great ideas can be just what a newsroom needs to up its game. Want to chat about your career questions? Email me at cristina.silva@globe.com with the subject “Ask the Recruiter.” Questions can be published with your name or anonymously. Whether you’re searching for your first newsroom job or your next leadership role, the Media Job Board features hundreds of opportunities from news organizations across North America. This kind of journalism doesn’t fund itself. Poynter helps you understand what’s happening in the media industry and how it shapes the news you read every day. Donations power our nonprofit newsroom and keep stories like these free for everyone. DONATE Tags: Ask the Recruiter , career advice , Career Development , job creation , journalism careers , journalism jobs , newsroom innovation , newsroom jobs , newsroom leadership , professional development Cristina Silva Cristina Silva is The Boston Globe’s Managing Editor for Operations and Standards, where she helps lead newsroom AI strategy, editorial standards and partnerships. Previously, she&hellip; Cristina Silva Top business leaders at Poynter’s Tampa Bay Times to depart at end of 2026 CEO and Chairman Conan Gallaty and President and COO Bala Sundaramoorthy leave as part of a restructuring November 16, 2026 Jennifer Orsi Opinion | A wild weekend of travel delays, explanations and excuses After a nationwide flight shutdown stranded travelers, Trump stayed publicly silent for nearly two days November 16, 2026 Tom Jones How Ohio journalists exposed dangerous dog laws — and helped change them At the heart of the award-winning investigation was a commitment to earning the trust of people who had survived devastating dog attacks. November 16, 2026 Amaris Castillo After standing up for his ‘60 Minutes’ colleagues, Scott Pelley wants to explore the ‘triumph of truth’ ‘When autocrats around the world and through history attempt to take away all of the people's rights, they always start with the press’ November 15, 2026 Angela Fu Here’s what a Democratic-controlled Congress could do to protect press freedom Congressional investigations could put the administration’s attacks on the press under scrutiny, while bipartisan legislation offers a path to reform November 13, 2026 Angela Fu Back to News Comments Cancel reply You must be logged in to post a comment. This site uses Akismet to reduce spam. Learn how your comment data is processed. Become a Poynter Member Don't miss out on a year of learning. JOIN TODAY Already a Member? Log in Start your day informed and inspired. Subscribe Get the Poynter newsletter that's right for you. Media Jobs News Editor Director of Advertising DIGITAL SALES STRATEGIST BROADCAST JOURNALIST: ARTS & CULTURE Deputy Managing Editor- News Director of Technology Chair and Professor of the Practice Publisher and Chief Executive Officer - Connecticut Mirror / CT Mirror News Editor, The Stranger - Seattle, WA (98122) Group Editor - Columbia, MD (21045) Search Media Jobs Advertise a job &times; This kind of journalism doesn’t fund itself. Poynter helps you understand what’s happening in the media industry and how it shapes the news you read every day. Donations power our nonprofit newsroom and keep stories like these free for everyone. DONATE",
