      "id": "73c44cfc0a1acfd07ab2f9f7b3a4fe14abb2fbf0a6e61ce591ccf03f14893b0c",
      "title": "The Mississippi Free Press Is Using Journalism to ‘Make Democracy Work a Little Better’",
      "url": "https://www.mississippifreepress.org/scott-colom-flips-forrest-county-named-for-ku-klux-klan-founder-nathan-bedford-forrest",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-16T19:27:34.535Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://www.mississippifreepress.org/scott-colom-flips-forrest-county-named-for-ku-klux-klan-founder-nathan-bedford-forrest/",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "Scott Colom Flips County Named for KKK Founder Nathan Bedford Forrest",
      "pageDescription": "Mississippi Democratic U.S. Senate candidate Scott Colom has flipped Forrest County, named for Ku Klux Klan founder Nathan Bedford Forrest.",
      "evidenceText": "Scott Colom Flips County Named for KKK Founder Nathan Bedford Forrest Close Search for: Search Close Skip to content Mississippi Trusted Elections Liveblog Democratic U.S. Senate candidate Scott Colom (inset right) won a majority of votes in Forrest County, a typically conservative Mississippi county named for Ku Klux Klan founder Nathan Bedford Forrest (inset left), in the Nov. 3, 2026, general election. MFP Photo by Ashton Pittman/Public Domain/MFP Photo by Rogelio V. Solis Mississippi Democratic U.S. Senate candidate Scott Colom has flipped Forrest County, a once reliably Republican County named for Ku Klux Klan founder Nathan Bedford Forrest. With 97% of votes counted in Forrest County, Colom led Republican incumbent U.S. Sen. Cindy Hyde-Smith by 54%-44% as of Thursday, Nov. 5. That represents a 29-point swing toward Democrats in a county that Donald Trump carried in 2024 by a 58%-40% margin. Colom, who currently has a narrow lead among votes counted, would be the first Black U.S. senator from Mississippi since 1881 during the Reconstruction era. Nathan Bedford Forrest, a slave trader and Confederate general from Tennessee, served as the first grand wizard of the KKK during the Reconstruction era.  Sign up for our FREE email newsletter today! Unlock exclusive, in-depth stories that go beyond headlines. Forrest County includes the majority-Black city of Hattiesburg, the home of the University of Southern Mississippi. In 2020, a county-wide vote to remove a towering Confederate monument next to the Forrest County Courthouse in Hattiesburg failed in 51% to 49% vote . About 85% of voters who live in the college town, though, voted to get rid of it. The United Daughters of the Confederacy erected the monument in 1910 as part of a propaganda campaign to perpetuate the Lost Cause myth. Ellie Dahmer (front right at the base of the statue) looks up joyfully after pulling the veil off a statue of her husband, Vernon Dahmer, in downtown Hattiesburg on Jan. 6, 2020. Photo by Ashton Pittman The Confederate monument sits opposite a monument erected in 2020 to honor slain local civil rights activist Vernon Dahmer . It features his words, “If you don’t vote, you don’t count.” In 1966, Dahmer fought the Ku Klux Klan, who had firebombed his home, as his wife and children escaped. He later died from his injuries. His wife, Ellie Dahmer, went on to serve as a Forrest County election commissioner. Forrest County has a rich civil rights legacy . In Hattiesburg, numerous civil rights groups organized in the 1960s to fight for voting rights, in a county where an ardent Dixiecrat circuit clerk forced Black voters to answer absurd questions before allowing them to vote like, “How many bubbles are in a bar of soap?” During the Freedom Summer of 1964, the “Freedom House” on Hattiesburg’s Mobile street served as a hub of civil rights activity, with seven freedom schools that taught thousands of Black students about petitioning their government and other topics not covered in Black public schools in segregated Mississippi. In recent years, vocal residents of Forrest County have pushed to have it renamed, with some suggesting simply dropping one of the r’s and naming it Forest County. Read the latest from Mississippi’s 2026 U.S. Senate election results at our liveblog here . Mississippi Free Press stories are always free because we believe everyone should have access to quality journalism. Donations from readers like you make that possible. Please click here to give to help sustain and grow vital, people-over-power journalism for Mississippians . Readers can sign up for our free newsletters here . Democracy Needs Local Journalism. Local Journalism Needs You.  Democracy is a system, and people need reliable information to take part in it. The Mississippi Free Press reports on the systems shaping life across our state, asks hard questions and follows the facts wherever they lead.  Your support makes this work possible. Donations help fund reporter salaries, on-the-ground reporting, public-records requests, fact-checking and coverage of the issues affecting Mississippians. Our stories are free to read because we believe everyone should have access to ethical, independent journalism rooted in accountability and truth.  Thanks to Charles Cadwell and Mary Schapiro, your donation is matched, up to $5,000. Your gift can help us make the most of this match and keep trusted local journalism free and accessible. A monthly gift is one of the strongest ways to sustain this work. Recurring support helps us put more reporters in more communities and keep trusted local journalism accessible to everyone. If you believe Mississippi deserves journalism grounded in integrity and service, support the Mississippi Free Press today. Recent elections Stories Editor’s Note | Remembering Those Who Paved the Way for Scott Colom’s Victory in Mississippi Michael Watson Asks Whether He Should Sue Experian Over Voter Roll Issues in Deleted Post Colom Appears Likely to Have a Good Relationship With Sen. Wicker. Mississippi’s Last Black Senator Wasn’t So Lucky. ‘Mississippi Matters’: Scott Colom’s Victory Challenges Assumptions About the Magnolia State, Could Herald a New Era Ashton Pittman News Editor and Journalist ashton@mississippifreepress.org Award-winning News Editor Ashton Pittman, a native of the South Mississippi Pine Belt, studied journalism and political science at the University of Southern Mississippi. Previously the state reporter at the Jackson Free Press, he drove national headlines and conversations with award-winning reporting about segregation academies. He has won numerous awards, including Outstanding New Journalist in the South, for his work covering immigration raids, abortion battles and even former Gov. Phil Bryant’s unusual work with “The Bad Boys of Brexit\" at the Jackson Free Press. In 2021, as a Mississippi Free Press reporter, he was named the Diamond Journalist of the Year for seven southern U.S. states in the Society of Professional Journalists Diamond Awards. A trained photojournalist, Ashton lives in South Mississippi with his husband, William, and their two pit bulls, Dorothy and Dru. More by Ashton Pittman One reply on &ldquo;Scott Colom Flips Forrest County, Named for Ku Klux Klan Founder Nathan Bedford Forrest&rdquo; Pingback: A Black Man Just Flipped A Mississippi County Named After The KKK’s Founder - The New York Beacon Comments are closed. Search for: Search Recent Stories After 6 Years of Petitioning, Confederate Statue at Claiborne County Courthouse Relocated to Grand Gulf by Heather Harrison University of Mississippi Investigating Women Who Made Racist Gestures Outside Black Student’s Home by Ahmer A. Khan Clarksdale Municipal School District Encourages Mask Use as Respiratory Illnesses Rise by Torsheta Jackson Nuestro Arte: Hattiesburg Gallery Invites Hispanic Artists to Showcase Work, Share Culture by Rosainys Almonte Link Copy link",
