      "id": "8b28839e368bf6db9e38fd257e502c5f8353bf89923e80b8d53d7980a3a3b6ca",
      "title": "The Mississippi Free Press Is Using Journalism to ‘Make Democracy Work a Little Better’",
      "url": "https://www.cjr.org/the-interview/mississippi-free-press-using-journalism-to-make-democracy-work-a-little-better-ashton-pittman-midterms-hyde-smith-colom.php",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-16T19:27:34.535Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://www.cjr.org/the-interview/mississippi-free-press-using-journalism-to-make-democracy-work-a-little-better-ashton-pittman-midterms-hyde-smith-colom.php",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "The Mississippi Free Press is using journalism to “make democracy work a little better.”",
      "pageDescription": "Ashton Pittman on the groundwork that prepared journalists to cover a deadlocked US Senate race.",
      "evidenceText": "The Mississippi Free Press Is Using Journalism to ‘Make Democracy Work a Little Better’ - Columbia Journalism Review b The Interview Courtesy of Ashton Pittman b Sign up for the daily CJR newsletter . After most elections, Ashton Pittman, the news editor of the Mississippi Free Press , wraps up the paper’s results coverage by Wednesday night. But as updates trickled in on the slim margin between Cindy Hyde-Smith, the incumbent GOP senator, and Scott Colom, her Democratic challenger, in last week’s midterms, Pittman and a team of six reporters were still tracking ballot counts more than a week later. In a state that has not elected a Democrat to the United States Senate in forty-four years, the race remains too close to call.  For years, the Mississippi Free Press , a Jackson-based nonprofit news site, has been digging into the minutiae of the state’s county-based election system. Since 2020, when COVID-19 altered voting habits, Free Press reporters have been tracking polling-place location changes every state and federal election cycle; this year, they found eighteen incorrect locations listed in state voter resources. The outcome of this year’s Senate race hinges on affidavit ballots, also known as provisional ballots, given to people who show up to vote but whose eligibility cannot be immediately verified. Since the polls closed, Mississippi Free Press journalists have deployed their granular knowledge of the state’s decentralized system to track affidavit votes.  In doing so, Pittman explained, they’re not just watching the outcome of the midterms; they’re discovering which voters were given affidavit ballots and how county officials are managing affidavit votes. “Our approach to covering politics in Mississippi is less about covering politics than it is about covering democracy,” Pittman told me. “We focus a lot on voters and on the experience of voters.” Our conversation has been edited for length and clarity. EH: From pretty early on election night, it was clear that this race was going to be tight. What have you and your reporters been doing and seeing around Mississippi since Election Day? AP: Me and a team of reporters have been trying to call all eighty-two counties across the state and asking, “Hey, can you tell us how many affidavit ballots you have, how many are outstanding, how many have been rejected?” That’s helped us figure out what’s still out there, what is left to be counted, and also to try to identify patterns, like which counties have rejected more affidavit ballots, so we can, later on, try to figure out, “Why are people here being rejected more often than people in this other county?”  People are asking, why is Mississippi so slow at counting the votes? It always takes ten days for all the counties to finish and certify their elections, and it always takes time to count the affidavits and verify them. Usually people don’t pay attention to it because typically, before you ever get to counting affidavit ballots, you know what the outcome of an election is in Mississippi. I think it’s important for people to know that this is how the system works. There’s nothing unusual about what’s happening here.  We’ve seen different estimates on how many ballots remain to be counted. I’ve seen them ranging from fourteen thousand to twenty-five thousand at one point. Why is it so difficult to know how many ballots are outstanding? Mississippi is a bottom-up state where local election officials run and administer the elections. The secretary of state’s office does not know how many affidavit ballots there are, and it’s not really involved with that process of counting or processing them. So you have eighty-two different counties, and they may have eighty-two different systems, eighty-two different ways of doing things. There are a few counties that, the day after the election, had affidavit ballots counted, including the totals for each of the candidates. And there are some that, as of the weekend, had not even begun to actually count them.  Hinds County, for some reason, has not been able to tell us how many they have. Part of what’s really significant is Hinds County is the biggest county in the state. A lot of people are trying to look at how many affidavit ballots Hinds County had in past elections. The problem is that there was kind of a curveball thrown into this. The secretary of state implemented a system using addresses from Experian credit report data to basically move voters who get flagged in that system to inactive status. When you’re moved to inactive status, if you show up to vote, you’re not going to be on the polling-place list, so you have to cast an affidavit ballot. This new system with Experian was implemented ahead of this election. It wasn’t used in the last one, so we don’t know: Is the Experian system going to change how many people had to cast affidavit ballots in Hinds County? Part of the uncertainty is we’re not sure that we can judge based on what percentage of people had to cast affidavit ballots last time, so that’s why there are these extremely different estimates. In this very decentralized system here in Mississippi, how has your team approached reporting on ballot counts? We’re going off what the Associated Press has, but we’re also calling those local circuit clerks and election commissioners ourselves. In the counties where we have talked to people who have given us those breakdowns, we’ve found that Scott Colom wins by a much wider margin of affidavit ballots than he does overall. There are some counties where he’s losing overall, but in the affidavit ballots, he does really well. Aside from just the electoral horse race of it, more importantly, it shows us that Colom voters—who tend to be Black and younger—appear more likely to be flagged in this system and moved to inactive status, or for some other reason to have to cast an affidavit ballot. The Mississippi Free Press has been expanding its regional bureaus around the state. How has that network shaped your election coverage? It’s actually made a pretty significant difference. In 2020, we started trying to figure out which polling places have changed, and if polling-place information in the statewide system is accurate. When we started, it was just two of us making calls to the circuit clerks in eighty-two counties, and to election commissioners and supervisors, trying to figure this out. We thought we were going to easily put together a list of polling places that had changed in the pandemic. Then we found out that this system is quite arcane and confusing. At times, you might have to make six or seven calls to multiple different officials in the same county. Our geographic expansion has helped a lot. What we’ve been able to do this year with the polling-place project is get our reporter on the Gulf Coast to make these calls to the Gulf Coast counties, and our Delta reporter to make these calls to the Delta counties. The polling-place project also stands out to me for just how ground-level that reporting is. How did that project influence your team’s ability to do detailed, county-by-county reporting since the election? Just the fact that we already have that experience and know the process of working the phones to call these circuit clerks and election commissioners made it a lot easier for us to deal with trying to find out about affidavit ballots now. Because eighty-two counties is a daunting task, even with a team of seven people around the state.  Are there any particular insights that Mississippi Free Press journalists have reported since election night that you think really capture what’s happening in the state right now? One of the counties Scott Colom flipped, that typically votes for Republicans, is Forrest County , named for Nathan Bedford Forrest, who was the first grand wizard of the Ku Klux Klan. Just looking at the fact that a Black man running in Mississippi flipped that county, I think that is significant because it shows that places can change.  You mentioned how this election is upending a lot of political assumptions. You’ve also noted that, going back to 2018, we’ve seen some of these long-held ideas about Mississippi being challenged in the actual vote tallies. Do you take any insights from that about how journalists can and should be covering politics here in Mississippi? Right now, we’re covering the numbers; we’re covering the returns. What we don’t do before the election is we don’t spend time putting out stories about polls: who’s up today, who’s down today, who’s attacking whom, who put out a zinger. We’re putting out pieces that let people know, hey, here’s how you vote absentee, or here’s how you get a voter ID because that’s required in Mississippi. Letting people know if their polling places change, because there’s literally nobody who does that. There is no list on the secretary of state’s website, at least most years, that tells you if your polling place has changed. And a lot of the reason is that the secretary of state does not know about all polling-place changes. It’s kind of like looking for those gaps in our election systems, and figuring out how we can help fill in those gaps, how we can use journalism to help empower voters and to make democracy work a little better. Has America ever needed a media defender more than now? Help us by joining CJR today . Elizabeth Hewitt is a journalist who lives in Brooklyn. She previously reported on Congress and criminal justice for VTDigger.org. Follow her on Twitter @emhew . More from CJR Is It Crazy to Go to Journalism School? A student reflects on leaving the law to pursue his writing dreams. By Megan Greenwell   Crooked Con’s Hottest Takes Meeting up with podcasters, creator-journalists, and political operatives against a dominant blue backdrop. By Carolina Abbott Galvão   Misread Mandate Trump never had the support media moguls seemed to think he did. What’s next for the outlets that capitulated? By Jem Bartholomew   Political Journalists in Disarray Midterm missteps, a notable apology, and a sturgeon rescue. By Bill Grueskin   The voice of journalism, since 1961 About Mission Masthead Privacy Policy Contact Support CJR Become a Member Donate Advertise Contact Us Copyright 2026, Columbia Journalism Review",
